-- title:
--   Half-plane cuts of convex domains are convex
-- statement:
--   A finite vertex set $D$ is admissibly convex if there are integers $\ell_k<h_k$ ($k=0,1,2$) such that $v\in D$ exactly when $\ell_k<t_k(v)<h_k$ for the three tiling coordinates $t_0=2y/\sqrt3$, $t_1=x-y/\sqrt3+\frac12$, $t_2=x+y/\sqrt3+\frac12$ of the position $(x,y)$ of $v$. The star faces of $D$ are the hexagons adjacent to some vertex of $D$; following the boundary of this face set with the star faces on the right gives the star boundary walk. If $D$ is admissibly convex, then for every direction $k$ and integer $A$ the cut domain
--   $$
--   \mathrm{upperCut}(D,k,A)=\{v\in D:\mathrm{boxIndex}_k(v)<A\}
--   $$
--   is again admissibly convex.
--
--   So restricting a convex domain by a lattice half-plane preserves convexity.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/Combinatorics/HoneycombCorridor/ConvexCuts.lean#L30-L52

